Troubleshoot and fix a Docker volume permission denied failure using an entrypoint script with `gosu`.

Requirements:
- Located in `/app`, you will find `app.py` and `Dockerfile`.
- Build the initial image (`docker build -t myapp:latest /app`).
- Run a container mounting host directory `/tmp/host_data` to `/app/data`:
  - `docker run -d -v /tmp/host_data:/app/data --name app_container myapp:latest`
- Observe `docker logs app_container`. The app crashes with:
  `FATAL: [Errno 13] Permission denied: '/app/data/app.log'`
- This occurs because host volumes mounted into `/app/data` are owned by `root`, while the app runs as unprivileged user `appuser` (UID 1001).
- Solve the permission issue using an entrypoint script:
  1. Create `/app/entrypoint.sh` (or `/usr/local/bin/entrypoint.sh`) that starts as `root`.
  2. Inside `entrypoint.sh`, fix directory permissions/ownership on `/app/data` (e.g., `chown -R appuser:appuser /app/data`).
  3. Use `exec gosu appuser "$@"` to drop privileges from `root` to `appuser` and execute the application command.
  4. Update `/app/Dockerfile` to copy `entrypoint.sh`, make it executable (`chmod +x`), and set `ENTRYPOINT ["/usr/local/bin/entrypoint.sh"]`.
- Rebuild the image (`docker build -t myapp:latest /app`) and run the container with the mounted volume (`docker run -d -v /tmp/host_data:/app/data --name app_container myapp:latest`).
- Verify `docker logs app_container` displays:
  `SUCCESS: Data written to volume successfully!`
